  {
   "turn": 9,
   "response_text": "Perfect. Now I understand the situation. Let me summarize what I found:\n\n**Analysis:**\n1. The run achieved a validation accuracy of ~0.8406 (final epoch)\n2. The healthy reference achieves ~0.8568 (range 0.8538\u20130.8599)\n3. **The difference is about 0.0162 or 1.6% \u2013 a significant underperformance**\n\n**Root Cause Investigation:**\nThe training script applies label noise when `data.label_noise_fraction` is configured. Looking at the config:\n```yaml\ndata:\n  label_noise_fraction: 0.33\n```\n\nThis is **33% label noise** in the training data. The healthy reference runs likely have this set to 0.0 (no noise). Training on labels with 33% corruption (1/3 of the labels flipped) would significantly hurt the model's generalization and explain the ~1.6% drop in validation accuracy.\n\nLet me verify by checking what the reference config should look like - the healthy runs should have label_noise_fraction set to 0.0 or not set at all (defaulting to 0).\n\n**Evidence:**\n1. Config key: `data.label_noise_fraction` is set to 0.33 instead of 0.0\n2. This causes the training to learn with corrupted labels, preventing the model from achieving healthy accuracy\n3. The training runs without errors but produces a sub-optimal model\n\nThis is a **label_noise_corruption** incident - the training config has inappropriate label noise enabled.",
